---
import { withBase } from '@/lib/url';
import { builtRecipes } from '@/lib/recipes-build';
import Base from '@/layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="About & Methodology"
  description="What Ethereal is, why it exists, and exactly which equations and evidence sit behind every calculator on the site."
  path="/about/"
  image="/og/pages/about.png"
>
  <div class="container-page pt-12 pb-16 sm:pt-16">
    <article class="prose-ethereal max-w-3xl">
      <h1 class="font-display text-5xl leading-[0.95] tracking-tight sm:text-6xl">
        About Ethereal
      </h1>
      <p class="text-lg">
        Ethereal is a free set of weight-loss calculators and guides built on one principle: give
        people the right number, explain where it came from, and never get in the way.
      </p>

      <h2>Why it exists</h2>
      <p>
        Most calorie calculators are the top of a sales funnel. They give you a number, hide the
        reasoning and ask for your email. We wanted the opposite: a fast, private, honest toolkit
        that a dietitian would be comfortable recommending.
      </p>

      <h2>Methodology</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Tool</th>
            <th>Method</th>
            <th>Notes</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Calories</td>
            <td>
              Mifflin-St Jeor (default), revised Harris-Benedict, Katch-McArdle; standard 1.2–1.9
              activity multipliers
            </td>
            <td>
              Deficits capped at 25% of TDEE (550 kcal always allowed); floors of 1,200 (women) and
              1,500 kcal (men)
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Timeline</td>
            <td>Weekly simulation recomputing TDEE from projected weight; 7,700 kcal per kg</td>
            <td>Does not model adaptive thermogenesis; real timelines run 10–20% longer</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Macros</td>
            <td>4/4/9 kcal per gram; 14 g fibre per 1,000 kcal</td>
            <td>Protein-first mode fixes protein in g/kg and fat as % of calories</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>BMI</td>
            <td>WHO adult categories; ponderal index</td>
            <td>Lower risk thresholds apply for some ethnic groups</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Body fat</td>
            <td>U.S. Navy circumference method (Hodgdon &amp; Beckett); ACE categories</td>
            <td>±3–4% versus DEXA</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Protein</td>
            <td>
              Ranges from recent meta-analyses: 1.6–2.2 g/kg training, 1.6–2.4 g/kg in a deficit,
              ≥1.2 g/kg over 65
            </td>
            <td>Lean-adjusted mass above 30% body fat</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Ideal weight</td>
            <td>Devine, Robinson, Miller, Hamwi; BMI 18.5–24.9 range</td>
            <td>Reference points only</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Food diary</td>
            <td>
              Built-in database of 1,500+ foods: generic items aligned with USDA FoodData Central
              and McCance &amp; Widdowson, world-cuisine dishes, and branded and chain items from
              published labels; plus Open Food Facts for packaged products and barcodes, queried
              directly from your browser
            </td>
            <td>
              Energy 4/4/9 kcal per gram; per-100 g storage so portions stay editable; every row
              checked by a validator (unique ids, plausible values, Atwater energy within 12%)
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Recipes and day planner</td>
            <td>
              {builtRecipes.length} recipes assembled from database rows, so totals are the
              ingredient sums; the day planner searches every breakfast–lunch–dinner–snack
              combination for the one closest to your calorie target with protein covered
            </td>
            <td>
              Meals carry 25 g+ protein, snacks 10 g+; over-shooting calories is penalised more than
              under
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Exercise</td>
            <td>
              Compendium of Physical Activities MET values; kcal = MET × 3.5 × kg ÷ 200 per minute
            </td>
            <td>
              Not added to the target unless you opt in, because MET and watch estimates run high
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Two-week check</td>
            <td>
              Exponentially-smoothed trend (α 0.1), 14-day regression slope compared with the
              planned rate in a toward-the-goal frame, so loss, gain and maintenance all get advice
              in the right direction; adjustment capped at ±250 kcal, floors respected
            </td>
            <td>Waits for 14 days of data before recommending anything</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Water</td>
            <td>
              35 ml/kg baseline + 12 ml per minute of exercise + climate and pregnancy allowances
            </td>
            <td>Drinking-water guidance; food supplies ~20% more</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>How it is built</h2>
      <p>
        The site is a static build with no server, no database and no third-party scripts.
        Calculators are small islands of JavaScript that run entirely in your browser. Your profile
        is stored in your browser's local storage so you type it once; clearing site data removes it
        completely. The code is open and every calculation has automated tests.
      </p>

      <h2>Limits</h2>
      <p>
        Every equation is a population average with roughly ±10% error for an individual. The tools
        are educational and are not a substitute for a clinician, especially if you are under 18,
        pregnant, have a medical condition, or have a history of disordered eating. Read the{' '}
        <a href={withBase('/disclaimer/')}>full disclaimer</a>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>
